@page "/blogs/from-debt-to-discipline-managing-nuget-dependency-technical-debt-in-dotnet"
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(NugetTechnicalDebt)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                NuGet dependency work competes with feature work, so teams sometimes defer updates. The effort
                to catch up depends on factors such as API changes, package support, test coverage, and how
                much package-specific code has spread through the application; version distance alone does
                not predict the cost.
            </p>
            <p>
                <Highlight>NuGet dependency technical debt</Highlight> is the future maintenance cost of
                deferred updates, undocumented decisions, unreviewed transitive dependencies, and package
                APIs coupled to application code. This article focuses on making that work visible through
                inventory, documented deferrals, recurring review, and ownership.
            </p>
            <p>
                The earlier articles in this series covered how to find vulnerabilities, how to wrap and
                replace packages, and how to verify replacements with characterization and contract tests.
                This article covers the team practices that support those steps: documenting deferrals,
                scheduling dependency reviews, and assigning responsibility for findings.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <h4>Version Lag Can Increase Migration Work and Uncertainty</h4>
            <p>
                A gap across several major versions can require reviewing multiple migration guides and
                adapting code to breaking changes. The effort is not predictable from version count alone:
                inspect release notes, supported upgrade paths, dependency constraints, and tests before
                estimating the work.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
# Snapshot of a project that deferred upgrades for 18 months.
# Compare available versions; the output does not estimate migration effort.
dotnet list package --outdated

Project 'OrdersApi' has the following updates to its packages
   [net10.0]:
   Top-level Package          Requested  Resolved  Latest
   > LegacyCsvProcessor       2.1.0      2.1.0     5.0.1   # 3 major versions behind
   > Contoso.PdfRenderer      1.3.4      1.3.4     4.1.0   # 3 major versions behind
   > Acme.ReportingEngine     3.0.0      3.0.0     3.7.2   # minor — still manageable
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The inventory and advisory outputs show the state before the example's later remediation
                decision; the ADR records selecting <Highlight>2.4.2</Highlight>. This version gap is a prompt
                to review intervening changes, not proof that three separate
                migrations or a rewrite are required. Follow the publisher's documented upgrade path.
            </p>

            <h4>Package Types Expand the Change Surface</h4>
            <p>
                When third-party types spread into application or domain code, package API changes can affect
                many consumers. An application-owned contract can reduce that coupling, though introducing
                it requires changing current consumers.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Dependency sprawl in practice: LegacyCsvProcessor types have leaked
// from the infrastructure layer into the application and domain layers.
// Depending on the API change, several of these references may need updates.

// Infrastructure layer — expected
using LegacyCsvProcessor.Readers;
public class CsvOrderImporter { ... }

// Application layer — a warning sign
using LegacyCsvProcessor.Configuration;
public class ImportOrdersCommand
{
    private readonly CsvReaderOptions _options; // LegacyCsvProcessor type in app layer
}

// Domain layer — a red flag
using LegacyCsvProcessor.Models;
public class Order
{
    public CsvRow? SourceRow { get; init; } // LegacyCsvProcessor type in domain model
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                When <Highlight>CsvRow</Highlight> appears in a domain model, upgrading
                <Highlight>LegacyCsvProcessor</Highlight> is no longer an infrastructure concern — it
                is a domain-layer breaking change. The Anti-Corruption Layer pattern covered in the previous
                article in this series exists precisely to prevent this spread.
            </p>

            <h4>Transitive Dependencies Are Your Responsibility Too</h4>
            <p>
                A direct package can bring transitive dependencies into the resolved graph. If an advisory
                affects one of them, check the affected version range and the projects and target frameworks
                that resolve it. A package listing alone does not prove that the package is loaded at runtime.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
# Include packages that are not direct project references.
dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive

Project 'OrdersApi' has the following vulnerable packages
   [net10.0]:
   Top-level Package      Requested   Resolved   Severity   Advisory URL
   > LegacyCsvProcessor   2.1.0       2.1.0      High       https://example.invalid/advisories/legacy-csv

   Transitive Package                   Resolved   Severity   Advisory URL
   > Contoso.InternalXmlParser          1.0.8      Critical   https://example.invalid/advisories/xml-parser
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>Contoso.InternalXmlParser</Highlight> is not a direct entry in this project's
                <Highlight>.csproj</Highlight>, but it appears in the resolved graph. Verify which projects
                include it, inspect the advisory's affected range, and identify an upstream update or
                compatible remediation. The graph alone does not establish runtime use.
            </p>

            <h4>Undocumented Deferrals Are Harder to Triage</h4>
            <p>
                A package that is behind with a pinned version comment in the
                <Highlight>.csproj</Highlight>, an ADR explaining why, and an owner responsible for
                the upgrade plan is a documented deferral. A package that is behind with
                no comment, no record, and no owner — where the developer who chose to pin it left
                the organization — is difficult to triage because its context is missing. Documentation
                helps teams review a decision; it does not make an unsafe or overdue deferral acceptable.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>Step 1: Record Deferrals and Review Their Status</h4>
            <p>
                Use this framework as a team triage aid, not a universal debt score. A documented deferral
                still needs an owner, a review date, and an acceptable risk decision:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
  ┌──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┐
  │                          │ Documented                    │ Undocumented                      │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Deliberate decision      │ DOCUMENTED — review on the    │ UNRECORDED — document rationale  │
  │                          │ scheduled trigger date        │ and assign a review date         │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
  │ No decision made         │ UNASSESSED — assign an owner  │ UNASSESSED — triage and assign    │
  │                          │ and record the context        │ an owner                          │
  └──────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┘
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Record what remains unknown and assign someone to resolve it. Revisit documented decisions
                when their trigger date arrives or when support and advisory information changes.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 2: Document Every Deferral Decision with an Architectural Decision Record</h4>
            <p>
                An <Highlight>Architectural Decision Record</Highlight> (ADR) is a short Markdown file
                that captures a technical decision at the moment it is made — the context behind
                the decision, the options that were considered, the rationale for the choice, and the
                consequences that follow. For NuGet dependencies, an ADR preserves the reasons for a
                deliberate deferral. Commit ADRs alongside your code in a <Highlight>docs/adr/</Highlight>
                folder at the repository root.
            </p>
            <p>
                This illustrative ADR records an update decision. Replace its sample details with
                verified advisory information, your resolved package graph, and team policy:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-markdown">
# ADR-0004: Pin LegacyCsvProcessor to v2.4.2 and Defer v3 Migration

**Status**: Accepted
**Date**: 2026-08-15
**Owner**: @@jane.doe
**Review trigger**: 2027-02-15 OR when LegacyCsvProcessor v3.1.0 is released — whichever comes first

## Context

LegacyCsvProcessor v3.0 was released on 2026-07-01 with a breaking change to `ICsvReader`:
the `ReadAll&lt;T&gt;()` method changed from returning `IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;` to `IAsyncEnumerable&lt;T&gt;`,
requiring async rewrites across 14 call sites in OrdersApi.

On 2026-08-10, an advisory was reported for versions of LegacyCsvProcessor below v2.4.2.
The team reviewed the affected-version range, package usage, and available updates.

## Assumptions

- The advisory's affected-version range and remediation were checked against package release notes
- The async API rewrite is estimated at 3 developer-days; capacity is available in Sprint 42 (2026-10-01)

## Options Considered

| Option                          | Verdict  | Reason                                                           |
|---------------------------------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Upgrade to v3.0 immediately     | Rejected | Breaking API requires 3-dev-day async rewrite; mid-sprint       |
| Pin to v2.4.2 (patch)           | Accepted | Selects a version outside the affected range; validate graph |
| Remove the dependency entirely  | Rejected | CSV import is a core feature; no BCL equivalent                 |

## Decision

Pin `LegacyCsvProcessor` to `2.4.2` in the affected project and verify the resolved graph.
Schedule the v3.0 async migration for Sprint 42. Owner: @@jane.doe.

## Consequences

+ The selected version is outside the advisory's affected range, subject to graph verification
+ The planned v3 migration is documented, owned, and has a delivery date
- We accept one additional sprint of version lag (v2.4.2 vs v3.0)
- If the v3 migration slips past 2027-02-15, this deferral becomes uncomfortable debt
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Record the source of the advisory, affected range, selected version, verification evidence,
                owner, and review date. Revisit those assumptions when package or advisory information changes.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 3: Automate a Weekly Dependency Health Check</h4>
            <p>
                A scheduled health check makes newly reported advisories visible during routine review.
                Its timing does not guarantee that every advisory will be detected immediately. Restore the
                solution first when using an SDK before .NET 10. The vulnerability listing uses configured
                audit sources starting with SDK 9.0.300; earlier SDKs use package sources.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# scripts/nuget-health-check.sh
# Run from the repository root. Requires .NET SDK 9.0.200 or later for .slnx support.
# Works on Linux, macOS, and Windows (Git Bash or WSL).
# Usage: mkdir -p reports &amp;&amp; bash scripts/nuget-health-check.sh MyApp.slnx 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee reports/nuget-health-$(date +%F).txt
# If no solution path is given, the first *.slnx file in the current directory is used.

set -euo pipefail

if [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; then
    SOLUTION="$1"
else
    shopt -s nullglob
    SOLUTIONS=( *.slnx )
    if (( ${#SOLUTIONS[@]} == 0 )); then
        echo "Error: no solution file found. Pass the path as the first argument." &gt;&amp;2
        exit 1
    fi
    SOLUTION="${SOLUTIONS[0]}"
fi
TIMESTAMP=$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
mkdir -p reports

echo "========================================================"
echo " NuGet Dependency Health Report"
echo " Generated : $TIMESTAMP"
echo " Solution  : $SOLUTION"
echo "========================================================"
echo ""

echo "--- Packages with known CVEs (including transitive) ---"
dotnet list "$SOLUTION" package --vulnerable --include-transitive

echo ""
echo "--- Packages that have newer versions available ---"
dotnet list "$SOLUTION" package --outdated --include-transitive

echo ""
echo "--- Packages marked deprecated by their authors ---"
dotnet list "$SOLUTION" package --deprecated --include-transitive

echo ""
echo "========================================================"
echo " Report complete. Review all findings and create ADRs"
echo " for any deferral decisions before closing this report."
echo "========================================================"
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                This script reports findings; its package-list commands do not make a vulnerability-aware
                pass/fail decision. Run it on pull requests or a schedule for triage, and configure NuGet
                Audit diagnostics as CI errors separately when your policy requires a gate. Record a
                remediation or a documented deferral for each finding.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 4: Configure GitHub Dependabot for Continuous Monitoring</h4>
            <p>
                A scripted health check surfaces the state of your dependencies when you run it.
                <Highlight>GitHub Dependabot</Highlight> can report supported dependency alerts and propose
                updates when advisory and package data are available. Coverage and timing depend on repository
                configuration and data sources. Place a
                <Highlight>dependabot.yml</Highlight> file in your repository's
                <Highlight>.github/</Highlight> folder to enable it:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-yaml">
# .github/dependabot.yml
version: 2
updates:
  - package-ecosystem: nuget
    directory: /
    schedule:
      interval: weekly
      day: monday
      time: "06:00"
      timezone: UTC
    groups:
      # Bundle Microsoft.Extensions.* updates into a single PR
      # to avoid inbox noise from many individual package updates.
      microsoft-extensions:
        patterns:
          - "Microsoft.Extensions.*"
      microsoft-aspnetcore:
        patterns:
          - "Microsoft.AspNetCore.*"
    open-pull-requests-limit: 5
    reviewers:
      - octocat           # replace with your team's GitHub handle
    labels:
      - dependencies
      - nuget
    commit-message:
      prefix: "chore(deps)"
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The <Highlight>groups</Highlight> key can reduce the number of related pull requests.
                Review grouped updates for compatibility and test the resolved dependency graph before merge.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 5: Define a Team Upgrade SLA so Decisions Are Made Under Planning, Not Pressure</h4>
            <p>
                Without a written policy, every CVE becomes an ad-hoc negotiation: how bad is this,
                whose sprint does it land in, can we defer it? An <Highlight>upgrade SLA</Highlight>
                is a team-level agreement that defines response targets for advisory findings, segmented by
                severity. It converts reactive firefighting into a
                planned process.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
  ┌──────────────┬────────────┬───────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Severity     │ CVSS Score │ Response SLA              │ Deferral criteria                        │
  ├──────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Critical     │ 9.0 - 10.0 │ Remediate within 24 hours │ None — no deferral permitted             │
  │ High         │ 7.0 - 8.9  │ Remediate within 5 days   │ ADR required + lead sign-off             │
  │ Moderate     │ 4.0 - 6.9  │ Remediate within 30 days  │ ADR required                             │
  │ Low          │ 0.1 - 3.9  │ Remediate in next sprint  │ ADR optional if patch is available       │
  └──────────────┴────────────┴───────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                These timeframes are an example team policy, not a .NET or industry-wide standard. Keep the
                advisory severity label intact. Use affected versions, package usage, exposure, available
                fixes, and organizational requirements to prioritize remediation. Document the rationale and
                review date for any deferral.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 6: Assign an Owner to Every Dependency</h4>
            <p>
                Assign responsibility for reviewing dependency findings and updates. A person or team can own
                triage for a package or group of packages; define backup coverage so work does not stop when
                someone is unavailable.
            </p>
            <p>
                A lightweight ownership registry committed to your repository makes this explicit.
                A simple Markdown table in a <Highlight>docs/DEPENDENCY_OWNERS.md</Highlight> file
                is sufficient — it does not require tooling, and it is discoverable by anyone on
                the team:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-markdown">
# Dependency Ownership Registry

All NuGet packages with a direct dependency must have a named owner.
Owners are responsible for: monitoring CVE alerts, triage, ADR creation,
and backlog story ownership. Rotate ownership quarterly during sprint planning.

| Package                   | Current Version | Owner          | Last Reviewed  | Notes                              |
|---------------------------|-----------------|----------------|----------------|------------------------------------|
| LegacyCsvProcessor        | 2.4.2           | @@jane.doe     | 2026-08-15     | ADR-0004: v3 migration due 2026-Q4 |
| Contoso.PdfRenderer       | 1.3.4           | @@bob.smith    | 2026-06-01     | v2 migration blocked on PDF spec   |
| Acme.ReportingEngine      | 3.7.2           | @@alice.jones  | 2026-07-20     | Current; no open issues            |
| Microsoft.AspNetCore.*    | 10.0.3          | @@bob.smith    | 2026-07-01     | Grouped via Dependabot             |
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Review this file on a cadence that fits your team's policy. An old review date prompts a check
                of support and advisory status; it does not by itself show that the package is unsafe.
            </p>

            <h4>Putting It All Together: From Weekly Cadence to a Debt Backlog</h4>
            <p>
                The six steps above form a repeatable workflow. Here is how they combine in practice
                week to week:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
  Weekly NuGet dependency health workflow

  Monday morning (automated)
  ───────────────────────────
  1. Owners review any new Dependabot alerts from the weekend
  2. CI runs nuget-health-check.sh and posts the report as an artifact

  Monday planning (manual — 15 minutes)
  ───────────────────────────────────────
  3. Dependency owners review Dependabot alerts and the health report
  4. For each new finding, apply the SLA table to determine the required response window
  5. Classify each finding using the healthy-debt / rot framework:
       - Is there already an ADR? → Confirm the ADR still applies; update the review date
       - No ADR and deliberate deferral needed? → Write the ADR before closing the meeting
       - No ADR and no decision? → Assign an owner and create a backlog story today
  6. Dependabot PRs for non-breaking updates are merged or assigned to the relevant owner

  Sprint planning (quarterly)
  ────────────────────────────
  7. Review docs/DEPENDENCY_OWNERS.md — rotate ownership where needed
  8. Schedule any ADR-deferred migrations that have hit their review trigger date
  9. Retire ADRs for migrations that have shipped
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Record the outcome for each finding: a remediation change, a decision to defer with an owner
                and review date, or a note explaining why the advisory does not affect the resolved package.
            </p>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <p>
                NuGet dependency technical debt is not a sign that your team is doing something wrong
                — it is a normal consequence of shipping software in a world where packages evolve,
                vulnerabilities are discovered, and sprint capacity is finite. The difference between
                a team that manages it well and one that does not is not the size of the debt; it is
                the visibility and ownership of it.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Classify before you act</Highlight>: Use the deliberate-vs-accidental
                    and undocumented deferral framework to make missing context visible. A deferral still
                    needs an owner and review date.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Document every deferral</Highlight>: An ADR committed alongside your
                    code preserves its rationale, owner, and review date. Reassess the decision when those
                    assumptions or advisory details change.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Automate discovery</Highlight>: A weekly script using
                    <Highlight>dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive</Highlight>
                    combined with configured alerts helps surface known findings. Neither guarantees complete
                    or immediate coverage.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Define your SLA</Highlight>: A written upgrade policy converts reactive
                    firefighting into planned work. Define response targets that reflect organizational
                    policy and risk context; do not relabel advisory severity based on local exposure.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Assign ownership</Highlight>: Define clear responsibility for triage and
                    updates, with backup coverage for continuity.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                For the techniques that make safe upgrades possible once you have decided to act —
                wrapping strategies, Anti-Corruption Layers, characterization tests, and contract
                tests — see the earlier articles in this series starting with
                <a href="/blogs/how-to-find-nuget-dependency-vulnerabilities-before-they-find-you-in-dotnet">How to Find NuGet Dependency Vulnerabilities Before They Find You in .NET</a>.
            </p>
        </Summary>
    </ContentBody>
</Content>
